#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162125 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02f8da68-ea1e-5f20-8f1a-7a38508e1f1f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Cumulative Probabilities Given Values From the Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a00bd8fd-f769-59af-94e0-09a33a7f0ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159693} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f01753-ff51-58f7-8441-6a5e5784017e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159694} {:answer/id #uuid "ff617689-9710-59b7-a4d3-2e0e590b84d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159695} {:answer/id #uuid "788a5919-0823-5058-9809-3ef68712428b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159696} {:answer/id #uuid "79088064-09e8-522c-a841-790c3c06532b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2f01753-ff51-58f7-8441-6a5e5784017e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159694} :answer-field/id #uuid "f51fefa5-bdb5-52c1-9dfd-154bef0ce526" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56a68cea-c574-533e-9e2c-ea532c1a718c" :question/math-academy-id "q-145626" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(9,10)=0.65,\\,F(6,10)=0.31,\\,F(3,10)=0.12,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{3,6,9,12\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(3<X\\le 9,Y\\le 10).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/77/7790a013e37bb86c00b44b1c022d6597f9bbdd57616c48e7ee7dfe8ddf512c63.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/37/379f0690fdbe92b7fb8f66cce4f12b214a23c6299fb8df71edf56d37fc0387fe.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(9,10)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,10)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/90/90a6730747b59a9886acbdf21880ca6a7ec3266142522ea5b32f07090963a98d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X\\le 9,Y\\le 10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(9,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 9,Y\\le 10)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 10)}} \\\\  & =F(9,10)-F(3,10) \\\\  & =0.65-0.12 \\\\  & =0.53.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162131 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b97d9a7a-fa12-5d8d-8958-39625f117979" :knowledge-point/title "Further Finding Cumulative Probabilities Given Values From the Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f01c561e-5fd3-5431-aa89-b6c0a3ea800f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159729} {:answer/id #uuid "128c143c-1206-5a0d-92ce-dad3f6df6aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159730} {:answer/id #uuid "2cac8f41-fff7-5b34-ae38-570dd1faab05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159731} {:answer/id #uuid "3396fd2c-6299-532f-81b0-89d0d78d2a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159732} {:answer/id #uuid "72d5813b-32a6-53c1-b39a-47c432820dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72d5813b-32a6-53c1-b39a-47c432820dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159733} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2220de-b4ac-5e91-8f92-853a80a549e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4909f9a0-a8a3-5a67-aa19-15cc754b3e1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-145909" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(7,1)=\\frac{9}{16},\\,F(7,-2)=\\frac{1}{4},\\,F(3,1)=\\frac{1}{8},\\,F(3,-2)=\\frac{1}{16},\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(5\\le X\\le 8,-1\\le Y\\le 1).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/9e/9ebff161899c6f61af3dffc1a5a0069de14bafd9b138293a945138e2951d066d.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(7,1)$ and bottom-left corner at $(3,-2)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/e2/e25278ed6d9712d8d2d2cabda5d1d0dbbf10aa8193f1342e7b4b66b26f9154e3.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(7,-2)$ and $(3,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,-2).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\le X\\le 8,-1\\le Y\\le 1) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{bottom-right corner at }(7,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le -2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-left corner at }(3,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{bottom-left corner at }(3,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le -2)}} \\\\  & =F(7,1)-F(7,-2)-F(3,1)+F(3,-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162130 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b8dd2d-ecaa-58bf-baa3-72dc8f37523f" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Joint CDF Geometrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54feaaa8-dd22-5ec0-9f20-e02541fd09ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494489} {:answer/id #uuid "8a615a1e-0c9e-52a7-9428-2729a456062a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494628} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eb4b4d-0d8f-5334-bdd7-099229f155e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494710} {:answer/id #uuid "c50b7eba-5426-53e7-a693-b3a4082cd5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494779} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5a4b19-57de-5fe5-bab6-461309d9b799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c50b7eba-5426-53e7-a693-b3a4082cd5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494779} :answer-field/id #uuid "473ef6d8-60c9-5504-8ce5-d78f894c72f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb06fe3b-f0e6-5f9b-9f03-e38285ba59c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-146415" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{-1,0,1\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<-1,y>2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{-1,0,1\\}\\times \\{1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<-1$ and $y>2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/41/41e8a9d9ec73c9c10a73c4dcd8a8fe9051401d6ab31c8274fdf8f22adb2437da.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at least $-1$ and $x<-1,$ no possible value of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162125 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02f8da68-ea1e-5f20-8f1a-7a38508e1f1f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Cumulative Probabilities Given Values From the Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "213c512b-4d83-52c6-aea3-4aa799b73e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494383} {:answer/id #uuid "5226bb01-1d74-50d5-ae38-837109e0e401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494481} {:answer/id #uuid "52c9b21a-4477-5890-bc16-7ba55dac052a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494483} {:answer/id #uuid "67f944a8-f948-5604-bc8c-ae5256daf681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494524} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad4d637-0bcc-57fd-a92b-74aa8cebd839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5226bb01-1d74-50d5-ae38-837109e0e401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494481} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1436435-7d0e-595c-99fd-60d4245df591" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9cd0722-c129-5c15-8f19-958d5be79276" :question/math-academy-id "q-146618" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(9,10)=0.8,\\,F(9,4)=0.3,\\,F(6,10)=0.4,\\,F(6,4)=0.2,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{3,6,9,12\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(6<X\\le 9,4<Y\\le 10).$" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/77/7790a013e37bb86c00b44b1c022d6597f9bbdd57616c48e7ee7dfe8ddf512c63.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/65/6535c5bf152e8a9b09bf5522cdc442668add6c4cc7a0df4e07307f9931886a80.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(9,10).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(9,4)$ and $(6,10).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,4).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6<X\\le 9,4<Y\\le 10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(9,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 9,Y\\le 10)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(9,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 9,Y\\le 4)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 10)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(9,10)-F(9,4)-F(6,10)+F(6,4) \\\\  & =0.8-0.3-0.4+0.2 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162131 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b97d9a7a-fa12-5d8d-8958-39625f117979" :knowledge-point/title "Further Finding Cumulative Probabilities Given Values From the Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ee2c542-f613-5ede-8ccb-305ddcfbc168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494449} {:answer/id #uuid "5595a205-6820-5fcd-b0d1-66910f4baf36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494493} {:answer/id #uuid "b53d2705-bd93-5c90-aa2b-ac6668486ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494740} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fa6b80-a26f-5238-b751-494b1bf5de75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494742} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c04b39-0608-5124-9679-01b2add1f439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5fa6b80-a26f-5238-b751-494b1bf5de75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494742} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8f6e27d-ab4f-5fa8-b770-f3c479ac71cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16e811ad-5456-5081-b6e1-700ad3de1eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-146850" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(4,1)=0.8,\\,F(4,-1)=0.2,\\,F(2,1)=0.3,\\,F(2,-1)=0.1,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(3\\le X<5,0\\le Y<2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/62/6287f4884b335466bc7e1d6da17190f32d447f065e37a37e79a1aa48a6109415.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(4,1)$ and bottom-left corner at $(2,-1)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/d4/d4d76ca2e80bccda9cbcfa4e28c628dffcec62b3fb42d62f9315fce2623e12fe.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(4,-1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(2,-1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X<4,-1\\le Y<1) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le -1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le -1)}} \\\\  & =F(4,1)-F(4,-1)-F(2,1)+F(2,-1) \\\\  & =0.8-0.2-0.3+0.1 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
